by jprivet-dev at 2026-01-30T07:29:03Z

> Furthermore, are we certain that the two services will not be triggered simultaneously? This could lead to a conflict.

Indeed! I've checked the logic to ensure we avoid any conflict or race condition.

### Dependency Injection Configuration

As seen in the updated `security_csrf.php`, I have removed the `kernel.event_listener` tag from `SameOriginCsrfTokenManager`. By default, only the new `SameOriginCsrfListener` will be triggered on `kernel.response`. The Manager is no longer an active listener:

```php
return static function (ContainerConfigurator $container) {
    $container->services()
        // ...
        ->set('security.csrf.same_origin_token_manager', SameOriginCsrfTokenManager::class)
            ->decorate('security.csrf.token_manager')
            ->args([
                service('request_stack'),
                service('logger')->nullOnInvalid(),
                service('.inner'),
                abstract_arg('framework.csrf_protection.stateless_token_ids'),
                abstract_arg('framework.csrf_protection.check_header'),
                abstract_arg('framework.csrf_protection.cookie_name'),
            ])
            ->tag('monolog.logger', ['channel' => 'request'])

        ->set('security.csrf.same_origin_listener', SameOriginCsrfListener::class)
            ->args([
                abstract_arg('framework.csrf_protection.cookie_name'),
            ])
            /* NEW TRIGGER */
            ->tag('kernel.event_listener', ['event' => 'kernel.response', 'method' => 'onKernelResponse'])
    ;
};
```

### Idempotency

In the edge case where a developer manually calls the deprecated methods (or manually registers the Manager as a listener in their own config), the logic relies on `$response->headers->setCookie()`. Since both classes implement the exact same logic, the operation is idempotent: the second call would simply overwrite the cookie with the same value, causing no data corruption.

### Deprecation Notice

If this edge case occurs, the developer will immediately trigger the deprecation notice added to `onKernelResponse()`, warning them that the logic is now handled automatically by the Listener.

